\section{When the Assembler Cannot Know the Address}

Labels and symbolic operands make assembly independent of a final placement. The assembler knows the current section and offset; it usually does not know where another input section or a definition in another compilation unit will end up. John R. Levine's \emph{Linkers \& Loaders} distinguishes the tasks of combining sections, resolving symbols, and relocating their references. An assembler first tries to settle a fixup from information it already has. For example, the distance between two fixed offsets in one section may not depend on the section's base. A reference that cannot be settled becomes a relocation, recording the place to modify, the symbol or section, a relocation type, and an addend.

The type determines more than the size of the patch. One relocation writes an absolute address; another writes a displacement from the patch site; another refers to an entry in a linkage table. Object formats also differ in whether the addend is embedded in the instruction field or stored as its own record field. In ELF terminology, a \texttt{RELA} record contains an explicit addend; a \texttt{REL} record supplies no addend field. Keeping these distinctions in the intermediate object model avoids accidentally adding the same contribution twice.

\section{Worked Relocation: A RIP-Relative Load}

Take \verb|mov eax, DWORD PTR [rip + item]| in Intel syntax. Suppose its first byte is placed at address \texttt{0x1000}, and the final definition of \texttt{item} is at \texttt{0x2000}. The six instruction bytes consist of \texttt{8B 05} and a four-byte displacement starting at \texttt{0x1002}. The \emph{System V AMD64 Architecture Processor Supplement} specifies $S+A-P$ for \verb|R_X86_64_PC32|, where $S$ is the symbol value, $A$ the addend, and $P$ the place of the relocation field. The instruction instead measures from the \emph{next instruction}. Therefore the assembler supplies $A=-4$ for this layout. The linker computes
\[
  \mathtt{0x2000}-4-\mathtt{0x1002}=\mathtt{0x0FFA},
\]
and the instruction bytes become \texttt{8B 05 FA 0F 00 00}. Adding \texttt{0x0FFA} to \texttt{0x1006}, rather than to the opcode address or patch address, recovers \texttt{0x2000}.

Use a separately defined nonpreemptible symbol in a test fixture and inspect both the relocation before linking and the bytes afterward. Rearrange the final sections so the target is at a lower address and check a negative displacement as well. If the linker cannot encode the signed displacement, it must diagnose the failure unless a legal replacement sequence or layout change is available. Truncation is not a relocation strategy.

\section{Relaxation Changes the Layout}

A linker may sometimes turn a conservative sequence into a shorter one after learning the relevant placements or binding rules. Relaxation is not simply substituting a constant: a size change moves later symbols and can change alignment padding and other displacements. An implementation needs a convergence policy, rechecks of the ranges on the settled layout, and a way to keep section, symbol, and metadata offsets consistent. A PC-relative access to an address in a global offset table is also not automatically interchangeable with a direct reference to the definition. For a symbol that can be resolved to a different shared object at runtime, bypassing that indirection may change which definition is used. Check what the object format and target relocation promise before relaxing it.

\section{Rewriting Already Selected Code}

IR-level link-time optimization can choose instructions again from the linked program representation. Rewriting selected code has fewer semantic clues. Schwarz, Debray, Andrews, and Legendre's \emph{PLTO: A Link-Time Optimizer for the Intel IA-32 Architecture} illustrates how preserved relocations can help disassembly, flow analysis, and adjustment of addresses after code movement. Do not assume that a page marked executable contains only instructions; alignment and inline data can lie between regions of code. A transformation that moves a block must account for incoming references, including address tables, and for any metadata that refers to the block, such as unwind ranges. When the rewriter cannot establish its set of references, leaving the region alone is a valid outcome. Test rewritten layouts with address-taking and unwinding fixtures, not only direct branches that are easy to rediscover.

For a reproducible layout test, retain the input objects in order, linker version, script, options, and both the relocation dump and final map. A map alone can help explain a result but will not reproduce it. The dossier's \href{https://github.com/Chubek/TheTomeOfLangDev/blob/HEAD/dossier-on-modern-cc-design/chapters/44-hermetic-compilation-capsules.md}{Hermetic Compilation Capsules} proposes packaging such inputs and artifacts together.
